# nordtal.eu season 2 - deployment settings.
#
# This file is a reference, not a form. Nobody fills it in any more:
#
#   curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nordtal/season-2/main/deploy/nordtal.sh | bash
#
# That script asks for what only a person can know and writes it itself, into the installation
# directory it is run from. Afterwards it is ./nordtal.sh in that directory, and running it again
# lists what is set, lets one be changed and deploys.
#
# The script creates the environment file at STEWARD_ENV_FILE (mode 600), asks for the handful of
# values below that it cannot invent - the host name, the ACME address, the EULA, the bot token,
# the two halves of the Discord application, the guild and the admin role, and bunq if there is a
# bunq - generates the database password, the forwarding secret and the two Steward tokens, and
# leaves everything else to the service's own configuration, which the web interface can edit.
# Running it again shows a menu of what is set.
#
# So read this file to find out what a setting is called and what it does. Do not copy it to .env
# and work down it: a value typed here that the interface also owns is a value in two places, and
# one of the two is wrong from the next edit onwards.
#
# The environment file holds the Discord token, the bunq API key, the database password and the
# forwarding secret. It is gitignored and must never be committed. This repository is public.

# How to read this file
# REPLACE_ME   A value only you can supply. It is deliberately not a plausible-looking default:
#              every id below is validated, and REPLACE_ME fails that check by name -
#              "roles.access must be a Discord snowflake (digits only), was: REPLACE_ME" -
#              instead of starting a bot that writes into a channel nobody meant.
#
#              So a half-filled .env stops the stack with a message naming the one setting that
#              is still wrong. That is the whole design; do not replace REPLACE_ME with zeros to
#              "get past" it, because a row of zeros is a valid snowflake and would start.
#
# Settings that are more than a single value - the language list, the price list - are written
# out in full below, filled with REPLACE_ME. Edit them in place: the shape is the documentation.
#
# Anything commented out is optional and already has a working default in the config file that
# owns it. Uncomment only to change it. An empty value means "not set" and is the same as
# leaving the line commented, so `FOO=` never means "empty string".
#
# The Discord ids are snowflakes. Discord: Settings -> Advanced -> Developer Mode on, then
# right-click a role or channel -> Copy Role ID / Copy Channel ID.

# What runs
# db  = PostgreSQL      bot = the Discord bot      mc = proxy + the three Paper servers
# backup = the pg_dump sidecar. Its own profile rather than part of `db`, because it is the one
# service that still makes sense against a database this compose does not own - and the one you
# must not lose silently by dropping `db`.
# steward = the web interface and what it needs: steward-ui, steward-deployer and Caddy. Caddy is in
# `mc` as well, because it is also the guard in front of the game port - and its one configuration
# asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate for STEWARD_HOST either way. So running the network means
# running steward: STEWARD_HOST and STEWARD_ACME_EMAIL below are required on every installation.
#
# `steward-worker` is missing from this line because it has no profile: it is in every selection,
# always, including COMPOSE_PROFILES=bot. It is the only process that applies the schema and the
# only one that answers /update in Discord, so nothing else here can correctly run without it.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=db,bot,mc,backup,steward

TZ=Europe/Berlin

# Versions
# There is no version in this file at all, and that is the whole section. A version number written
# down here can only go stale against gradle.properties, which is the single source of truth for
# it.
#
# What runs is decided in two places, neither of them here:
#
#   gradle.properties     the version everything is built at, in the repository, once
#   the volumes           the jar each container actually runs, put there by steward-worker
#
# `docker compose run --rm steward-worker report` prints what is installed, and every container prints the
# jar it picked on every start. Nothing in a .env can answer that question, so nothing here tries.
#
# Where our own jars and the resource pack come from. Read by the worker.
SEASON_REPO=nordtal/season-2

# SEASON_VERSION, BOT_VERSION, SEASON_RELEASE, IMAGE_TAG, PACK_URL and PACK_SHA1 are deliberately
# absent and must not come back.
#
#   - SEASON_VERSION and BOT_VERSION only ever named the jar a local `docker compose build` copied
#     into an image. The Dockerfiles now find it by glob in build/libs, so a local build takes
#     whatever Gradle just produced instead of what a variable claims it produced.
#   - SEASON_RELEASE and IMAGE_TAG were the rollback levers, and a lever is a version number kept
#     outside gradle.properties. The worker follows the latest release and the images are `latest`;
#     a bad release is fixed by publishing a better one.
#   - A jcore environment override wins over the file and is never written back, so the worker
#     could not write a new hash into pack.yml while PACK_URL and PACK_SHA1 were set. pack.yml in
#     the proxy volume is the place, and `steward-worker bootstrap` writes it.

# steward-worker
# It runs all the time, and it never updates anything on its own. `serve` applies the schema at
# startup and then does nothing at all until somebody asks - from /update in Discord, from
# /smp update in game, or from one of these:
#
#   docker compose run --rm steward-worker report   resolve and report, changes nothing
#   docker compose run --rm steward-worker migrate  apply the database schema, nothing else
#   docker compose run --rm steward-worker bootstrap    migrate, then fetch and place the files
#
# It is the bootstrap of this deployment, not a tool used on a running one: it is the only process
# that applies the schema and the only one that puts jars in volumes, so without a run of `apply`
# there is no bot and no server. Its database credentials come from the same NORDTAL_DATABASE_* /
# POSTGRES_* values as everything else and need no lines of their own.
#
# Which release it follows is not a setting, here or in steward.yml. It follows whatever GitHub
# calls the latest release, which is /releases/latest and therefore skips drafts and pre-releases by
# GitHub's own definition. That is the wanted behaviour and also the one trap left: a release left
# as a draft is invisible, and "the update did not arrive" looks exactly like "nobody pressed
# Publish". Check the releases page before looking anywhere else.
#
# There is no platform version and no build pin in this file, and both are deliberate. The Minecraft
# version and Velocity's major are eu.nordtal.s2.common.Platform, compiled into every one of our
# jars, because a platform version is a property of the season rather than of an installation. There
# is no build pin either: what a server runs is the newest stable build, resolved by the worker and
# by the container's own entrypoint.
#
# Whether `steward-worker serve` fills empty volumes before it reports itself ready. Leave it on.
#
# This is what makes a deployment possible with nobody typing anything on the host: a Minecraft
# server refuses to start on an empty plugins folder, and a deployment that only pulls images has
# no way to run `docker compose run --rm steward-worker bootstrap`. With this on, the first start of the worker
# container downloads what is missing and only then writes the readiness marker.
#
# It cannot move a version. Only artefacts with nothing installed are fetched; anything that
# already has a jar keeps it, however old. So a restart of a running network finds nothing missing
# and does nothing at all, and upgrades stay a request somebody makes from Discord or in game.
#
# Turning it off means the servers refuse to start until an apply has run, and say so by name.
#STEWARD_WORKER_BOOTSTRAP=true
#
# Optional. GitHub allows 60 unauthenticated requests per hour per IP and a run makes two, so this
# only matters on a host that shares its address with something busier. Public read is enough -
# the worker never writes to GitHub.
#STEWARD_WORKER_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Stopping, starting and recreating - nothing to set
# The worker talks to the Docker socket that compose.yml mounts into it - reading every container's
# state and health, stopping and starting them - and hands the one thing a socket must not be used
# for, creating a container, to steward-deployer.
#
# If you are upgrading a host that has a .env from before, delete these lines from it. Nothing reads
# them and ARCANE_API_KEY is a live credential sitting in a file for no reason:
#
#   ARCANE_URL  ARCANE_API_KEY  ARCANE_ENVIRONMENT  ARCANE_PROJECT
#   ARCANE_REDEPLOY_PATH  ARCANE_RUNTIME_PATH  ARCANE_CONTAINER_PATH
#   ARCANE_UPDATES_PATH  ARCANE_UPDATE_SERVICES_PATH
#
# The `arcane:` block in a deployed steward.yml goes the same way, and does not need a hand: jcore
# drops a retired key with a WARN and a .bak rather than refusing to start.
#
# What the socket costs, stated rather than hidden: a container that can talk to /var/run/docker.sock
# can do anything the daemon can, and this is the container that downloads files from the internet
# and puts them where servers execute them. `:ro` on the mount does not help - it restricts the
# socket file, not the API behind it. The restraint is in the code (DockerOps reads, stops and
# starts, and refuses to create a container) and the boundary that matters is that steward-ui, the
# part an attacker reaches first, gets no socket at all.

# Server software: nothing to set, and no PAPER_VERSION, PAPER_BUILD, VELOCITY_VERSION or
# VELOCITY_BUILD belongs here.
#
# The version is a literal in compose.yml, taken from eu.nordtal.s2.common.Platform and asserted
# against it by :steward-worker's TopologyTest. The build is resolved: the worker installs the
# newest stable build into each server's .server/ cache, and when that cache is empty the entrypoint
# asks the Fill API for the same thing.

# Images
# All four nordtal images are pulled from ghcr.io/nordtal and none is built on the host.
# .github/workflows/release.yml pushes minecraft, steward-worker and discord-bot
# when a release is published, each under the release version and under `latest`.
#
# This is not cosmetic. A deploy pulls and never builds, so an image that
# exists only in a host's local Docker makes a deploy fail with:
#
#   failed to pull image ghcr.io/nordtal/minecraft:local: ... error from registry: denied
#
# `denied` is also what a private package answers, which is the second half of the same trap: a
# package under an organisation is private on its first push. All four have to be set to Public in
# their GitHub package settings, once, or the host needs a registry credential instead. The worker's
# drift check resolves all three against ghcr.io with no authentication at all, which is what makes
# an unpushed image the only unverifiable kind.
#
# There is no tag to set. All four are `latest` and nothing pins them; see the Versions section at
# the top of this file for why. A bad release is fixed by publishing a better one.

# One image somewhere else entirely - a locally built one, or a fork. This is the only override
# left, and it is a whole image reference rather than a version, which is what keeps it from
# reading like a supported way to run an old release.
#MC_IMAGE=ghcr.io/nordtal/minecraft:dev
#BOT_IMAGE=
#STEWARD_WORKER_IMAGE=
#BACKUP_IMAGE=

# The PostgreSQL image. 17-alpine is the version every integration test in this repository runs
# against; changing the major version is a database migration, not a config edit.
#POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:17-alpine

# Minecraft
# Accepting https://aka.ms/MinecraftEULA. Deliberately not defaulted anywhere in the image.
EULA=true

# Velocity modern forwarding. Generate once:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 24
#
# It reaches all four services from here and nothing has to be pasted into a config file: the
# proxy container writes it to /data/forwarding.secret, and each Paper backend gets it as
# PAPER_VELOCITY_SECRET, which Paper reads from the environment on purpose (PaperMC/Paper#10127).
#
# Paper does then write it into its own config/paper-global.yml, so the value does end up in the
# three backend volumes - rotating it means changing this line AND that one, per backend.
#
# A mismatch - which now means "one of the four containers did not get this variable" - refuses
# every login with "Unable to connect you to the backend server".
VELOCITY_FORWARDING_SECRET=REPLACE_ME

PROXY_BIND=0.0.0.0
PROXY_PORT=25565

# Where a client reaches this network from outside, host and port - the address somebody types into
# Minecraft, not a name that only resolves inside this stack. deploy/nordtal.sh
# asks for it, so a fresh installation has it before anything starts.
#
# What reads it: the proxy, and only when it moves a player between the two proxies during an
# update. A transfer hands the client an address and the client dials it itself, which is why the
# value cannot be worked out from in here.
#
# The port is not optional, even where a SRV record lets players leave it out: the transfer packet
# carries host and port and nothing resolves a SRV record on its behalf.
#
# Leaving it empty is a decision and not a fault: no transfer is offered, and an update takes the
# network down the way it did before there were standbys.
NETWORK_PUBLIC_ADDRESS=REPLACE_ME

# The standby proxy's port, on the same address as above. The `standby` profile is what starts
# proxy-standby, and only an update run selects it - so this port is open for the minute a swap
# takes and closed the rest of the season. It has to be open in the host firewall (TCP) for that
# minute, or a transferred player cannot connect and comes back to a proxy that is restarting.
#
# UDP is published on it too and voice chat still does not work there - see the long note at
# proxy-standby's ports in compose.yml. Velocity binds 25565 inside every proxy container, and
# Simple Voice Chat hands the client the port it is bound to, so a player on the standby is told to
# send audio to 25565 on this host - the other container's port. That costs the voice connection
# for the seconds a swap takes and nothing else.
#PROXY_STANDBY_PORT=25566

# Simple Voice Chat has no variable here, and that is the whole point of installing its Velocity
# plugin on the proxy. Voice audio is UDP; with the proxy plugin the network needs exactly one open
# UDP port, and it is PROXY_PORT's number on PROXY_BIND's address - compose publishes 25565/udp
# beside 25565/tcp, and the plugin works out which backend each player is on by itself.
#
# So: open UDP 25565 in the host firewall alongside TCP, and nothing else. No per-backend port, no
# per-backend voice_host, no editing voicechat-server.properties by hand. Setting PROXY_PORT to
# something other than 25565 moves only the Minecraft port; see the note in compose.yml.

# Heaps. Unmeasured starting points, not measurements - the SMP's is the one that matters and it
# should be set from what the real host measures.
PROXY_HEAP=1G
LIMBO_HEAP=1G
# How many players the network accepts, and the only player number in the whole deployment - there
# is nothing else to keep it in step with.
#
# The proxy enforces it at the login gate - where it can say why - and advertises the same number in
# the server browser. All three Paper backends are set to it as well, so a tab list shows the number
# the browser promised instead of one only the servers know about. Admins are exempt, so a full
# network can still be entered by whoever has to go and fix it; the backends let those same admins
# past their own cap, which is what keeps one number from costing anything.
#
# Changing it needs the backends restarted, not just the proxy: each Paper container writes its
# server.properties on start. `docker compose restart proxy limbo hunger-games smp`.
#
# There is no BACKEND_MAX_PLAYERS: a backend enforcing its own limit means "Server full" arrives
# after a successful login, a resource-pack download and a wait in the waiting room. Delete it from
# your .env if it is there; it does nothing.
#NETWORK_MAX_PLAYERS=500

# What the server browser shows, one MOTD per season phase.
#
# MiniMessage, so <gradient>, <#rrggbb> and <newline> all work - <newline> is how you get the second
# line every client shows. Placeholders are written in braces and are listed in network.yml, which
# is where these live and where the shipped defaults are; anything left unset here keeps the default
# from that file, and those defaults are real MOTDs rather than placeholders.
#
# These are plugin config values read at proxy start, not something seeded into velocity.toml once -
# a MOTD on a fresh volume only is a MOTD nobody can change afterwards.
#
# The name is always #4a63d8 and nothing else - the logo's own blue, lightened for a list the client
# paints almost black. BrandColourTest fails a phase that colours the name for itself again; the
# phase is what the second line is for.
#
# There are no glyphs here and there cannot be: the server browser draws the MOTD in the client's
# own font, before any resource pack has been fetched. A private-use code point renders as a box.
#
#NETWORK_MOTD_PRE_LAUNCH=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Season 2 opens in <white>{countdown}</white></gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_PRE_EVENT=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Hunger Games: <white>{hg-participants}</white> players in <white>{hg-teams}</white> teams</gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_START_EVENT=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Hunger Games: <white>{hg-alive}</white> of <white>{hg-participants}</white> still alive</gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_SMP=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Season 2 running - <white>{smp-milestones-done}</white> of <white>{smp-milestones-total}</white> milestones done</gray>
#NETWORK_MOTD_MAINTENANCE=<#4a63d8><bold>nordtal.eu</bold></#4a63d8><newline><gray>Maintenance - back shortly, the season is not over</gray>

HUNGER_GAMES_HEAP=4G
SMP_HEAP=6G

# The whole JVM command line for every Minecraft service, if the heap settings above are not
# enough. Replaces them completely, so a value here has to carry -Xms/-Xmx itself.
#JVM_OPTS=

# Which backends the proxy knows, and where a player lands. Server names must match
# proxy's gate.yml#server-* keys; hostnames are compose service names. Only used when
# velocity.toml does not exist yet - the file is seeded once and then belongs to the operator.
#VELOCITY_SERVERS=limbo=limbo:25565 hunger-games=hunger-games:25565 smp=smp:25565
#VELOCITY_TRY=limbo

# There is no SMP_EXTRA_PLUGIN_URLS: three URLs with three versions in them, maintained by hand in
# a file nobody opens between deployments, is what steward-worker exists to end. It resolves
# DisplayTags from its own repository's releases and PacketEvents from Modrinth, filtered to this
# Minecraft version and `paper`.
#
# They are still required: smp's paper-plugin.yml declares DisplayTags load: before, required:
# true, and PacketEvents is required under it. The guard is in entrypoint.sh, which refuses to
# start on an empty plugins folder.

# The world-generation datapacks, and the world Paper reads them from.
#
# Datapacks are server-global and are read only from <level-name>/datapacks/; there is no per-world
# datapack API. So this one folder feeds Nordtal, the farm world, the Nether and the End alike, and
# the farm world's nightly regeneration inherits the packs with nothing copied.
#
# They are fetched before the server starts, because worldgen registries are read once: a pack
# dropped in afterwards changes no terrain, and terrain is never re-rolled once it is on disk.
#
# Format: whitespace-separated <sha512>@<url> entries (a bare URL works and only warns). sha512
# rather than sha256, because sha512 is what Modrinth publishes and a pin nobody can re-derive is a
# pin that rots.
#
# LEVEL_NAME must agree with server.properties#level-name and with the smp plugin's
# config.yml#world-nordtal. It is the world that holds the built spawn, the world the datapacks
# live in, and the world every other one is created inside.
#
# The entrypoint writes it into server.properties on a volume that has none. On a volume that
# already has a level-name saying something else the container refuses to start: renaming does not
# move a world, it generates an empty one beside it. The message names both values and the two ways
# out.
#
# One exception: a volume still carrying Paper's own default `world` is repaired rather than
# refused, provided nobody has ever logged out in it - that is not a name anybody chose, it is what
# Paper writes when nothing tells it otherwise. `world` and its two dimension folders are deleted
# and the name corrected. Any other disagreement is still fatal.
# See deploy/README.md#first-start-seeding.
#SMP_DATAPACK_URLS=f5283548323149ecd3f218b8efbe03c1c582df3f40b6bb3b095ba9e4cac0c87108b98adabd6ebd3a8037ec239773a253f75b8bee7db52e6f5663bf9dc63c2ea3@https://cdn.modrinth.com/data/8oi3bsk5/versions/CzijfXJQ/Terralith_26.2_v2.6.4.zip cf026b67f4fc0f5ab15229995f11faf43fdd41b29777a6756151276e83da65f887fe03b6356c5011f8ba32371f98941778928e4258e81b4c97411a40af1635ca@https://cdn.modrinth.com/data/tpehi7ww/versions/CS77UwHE/Dungeons%20and%20Taverns%20v5.3.2.zip
#SMP_LEVEL_NAME=nordtal

# The seed Nordtal is generated from. It is set ONCE, when the world is first created, and then
# frozen for the whole season - so this is the one number here that cannot be corrected later
# by editing a file. The entrypoint writes it only while the world does not exist yet; against a
# volume that already holds one it compares and warns, and never re-rolls anything. "Does not exist
# yet" is <level-name>/level.dat and NOT the folder: the folder is created by the datapack fetch
# above before Paper generates anything.
#
# Terralith and Dungeons and Taverns decide what a seed actually produces, so this number describes
# the same landscape only with SMP_DATAPACK_URLS above unchanged. The hunger games world is
# hand-built and shipped as a folder, so its own level-seed is never consulted.
#SMP_LEVEL_SEED=1837371427

# The hand-built hunger games map. The plugin looks for HungerGamesSpec#world-name and disables
# itself when that world is not loaded, so this is what brings it up - and it is the folder
# deploy/README.md#getting-a-world-into-a-volume copies into.
#HUNGER_GAMES_LEVEL_NAME=hunger_games

# Where each server's plugins/ lives. Leave all four unset on a production host - the defaults are
# named volumes (mc-<service>-plugins), which is the whole point of them.
#
# They exist because plugins/ is the only part of a server that a person edits: every plugin's
# config.yml, smp's milestones.yml and sounds.yml, and the proxy's pack.yml. Keeping it out of the
# world volume means the nightly backup can cover the configs without copying four gigabytes of
# terrain, and it means a jar swap does not touch the world.
#
# Never point one into a checkout: a deployment that checks this repository out over itself deletes
# ignored files from the directory it writes, so the first checkout after a hand edit takes every
# deployed config with it and every server comes back writing fresh defaults with nothing saying so.
#
# A value with a `/` in it is a bind mount to Docker; a value without one is a volume name. That is
# the only difference, so setting one of these to a path is deliberate and is what the local stack
# does - see deploy/dev.env.example. steward-worker mounts the very same four expressions; if these
# are ever set by hand, both sides move together or the worker installs where no server reads.
#PROXY_PLUGINS=mc-proxy-plugins
#LIMBO_PLUGINS=mc-limbo-plugins
#HUNGER_GAMES_PLUGINS=mc-hunger-games-plugins
#SMP_PLUGINS=mc-smp-plugins

# PostgreSQL
# One database, five clients: the bot (which is the only one that migrates) and the four
# Minecraft services. They all get these credentials through compose.yml - there is nothing to
# fill in per plugin.
POSTGRES_DB=nordtal
POSTGRES_USER=nordtal
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=REPLACE_ME
# Loopback only. Everything that reads this database is in the same stack; the publish exists for
# a psql from the host. Backups do NOT go through it - the sidecar below reaches the database over
# the compose network.
POSTGRES_BIND=127.0.0.1
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# Database backup (profile `backup`)
# A pg_dump into the `postgres-dumps` volume, daily. Anything that copies a volume offsite belongs
# on that volume and never on `postgres-data`: a file-level copy of a running PostgreSQL data
# directory is torn, and it fails when somebody tries to restore it rather than now - see
# README.md#backups. steward-worker's own backup.volumes refuses `postgres-data` by name for the
# same reason. Everything here has a working default; nothing needs filling in.
#
# Time of day in TZ above, 24-hour. A value that is not HH:MM stops the container rather than
# quietly becoming "never".
#BACKUP_AT=04:00
# How many dumps to keep in the volume. Offsite retention is whatever copies /backups away, not
# this one's.
#BACKUP_KEEP=14
# Dump once at start-up as well. Leave it on: it turns a wrong password into an error you see
# while deploying instead of a gap you find at 04:00 tomorrow.
#BACKUP_RUN_ON_START=true
# Back up a database this compose does not run. Defaults are the POSTGRES_* values above.
#BACKUP_PGHOST=db.example.org
#BACKUP_PGPORT=5432
#BACKUP_PGDATABASE=nordtal
#BACKUP_PGUSER=nordtal
#BACKUP_PGPASSWORD=

# Point the whole stack at a database outside it: set these three, then drop `db` from
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. The hostname `postgres` in the default URL is this compose's own service.
# These three reach the bot and all four Minecraft services - compose.yml hands each process the
# same values under the prefix it reads (NORDTAL_SMP_DATABASE_*, and so on). There is no
# per-service override; if one ever needs different credentials, that is an edit to compose.yml.
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_JDBC_URL=jdbc:postgresql://db.example.org:5432/nordtal
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_USERNAME=nordtal
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_PASSWORD=

# The bot's pool and SQL logging. Only the bot reads these two.
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE=10
#NORDTAL_DATABASE_LOG_SQL=false

# Discord bot - credentials
# https://discord.com/developers/applications -> your application -> Bot -> Reset Token.
NORDTAL_BOT_TOKEN=REPLACE_ME

# bunq: the API key and the id of the monetary account that is polled and billed. The account id
# is a number, not a snowflake and not an IBAN.
#
# Optional, both together or neither. A season with no bank account is a season whose bot does
# everything except take money: no poll, no purchase, and a line at startup saying so. Half of it
# is refused, because that is always a setup that stopped in the middle.
#NORDTAL_BOT_BUNQ_API_KEY=
#NORDTAL_BOT_BUNQ_ACCOUNT_ID=
# Discord bot - the guild
# The one guild the bot manages. Every id below is a role or channel inside it.
#
# This is one of the two ids a deployment cannot do without - the other is roles.admin. deploy/
# nordtal.sh asks for both. Everything else in this section is a feature: leave it empty and the bot
# does not serve the thing it names, says which ones in one line at startup, and you pick them in
# Steward afterwards rather than here.
NORDTAL_ACCESS_GUILD_ID=REPLACE_ME

# roles
# access      strictly bot-owned: added and removed to match the database, so granting it by hand
#             holds only until the next reconcile. /grant-access is the way.
# donor       granted on a donation and never taken away, by anything. Safe to hand out manually.
# admin       read-only for the bot: mirrored into discord_user.admin, and that flag is what
#             authorises /phase set and the maintenance phase everywhere else. Not cosmetic.
# admin-ping  who gets mentioned in the admin channel. Grants no power; may be the same role.
# Only `admin` is required, and nordtal.sh asks for it: without it nobody is an admin, which means
# nobody can log in to Steward to fill the other three in. The other three are optional - no access
# role means nobody is given or taken one (the grants in the database are still correct and the
# login gate still reads them), no donor role means a donor is recorded and not decorated, no
# admin-ping means alerts are posted without a mention.
NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_ADMIN=REPLACE_ME
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_ACCESS=
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_DONOR=
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_ADMIN_PING=

# channels
# The only channel that is not per-language: everything a human may need to act on - unmatchable
# payments, payments on an expired reference, failed DMs, role errors, every link and unlink.
#
# Optional. Without it those alerts are written to the bot's log instead, at warn, which is where
# they can still be read - a deployment that has not made the channel yet must not be one that
# cannot start.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_CHANNELS_ADMIN=

# Discord bot - languages
# Every language the network speaks, and the four Discord objects each one needs. This is the
# only place they are configured; there are no per-language settings anywhere else.
#
#   tag                     lower case, the name of the .properties bundle in every module's
#                           messages/ directory, and the value stored in discord_user.locale.
#                           'en' is mandatory - it is what a missing translation falls back to.
#   role                    the role Discord's own onboarding assigns for this language.
#                           Read-only for the bot; no role at all means English.
#   contribution-channel    carries the buy-access message and its donation thank-yous.
#   link-channel            carries the account-link message.
#   hunger-games-channel    carries the Register message. A separate channel on purpose:
#                           registering for the start event is not buying access, and access is
#                           not required to play.
#
# A third language is an edit here plus <tag>.properties in every module - not a release. The
# order matters in one place only: it is the order the link-code disconnect screen prints.
#
# JSON, because this is a list of structures and .env has no other way to carry one. The quotes
# and the line breaks are both fine - docker compose reads a single-quoted multi-line value as one
# string.
#
# Every id in the block is optional, and each one that is empty switches off exactly the thing it
# names: no role, and no member is ever recorded as speaking that language; no contribution-channel,
# and there is no buy message and no public thank-you in it; no link-channel, no link message; no
# hunger-games-channel, no Register message. The tag is the only thing an entry must have. What is
# not lenient is a value that is present and is not a snowflake - `<#123>` pasted out of Discord is
# refused by name, because an unresolvable channel and an unconfigured one look identical from the
# outside and are not the same thing at all.
#
# "status-channel" wants a channel whose name the bot may rewrite - a voice channel is the usual
# shape, because Discord keeps a voice channel's name verbatim and lower-cases a text channel's -
# and the sidebar then carries the countdown to the opening and, after it, what the network is
# doing. Left empty, that language simply has no status channel and the bot renames nothing.
#
# The whole variable is optional too. Leave it out and access.yml's own default stands: `en` and
# `de`, with every id blank. That is a bot that starts, joins the guild and waits for somebody to
# pick the channels in Steward.
NORDTAL_ACCESS_LANGUAGES='[
  {
    "tag": "en",
    "role": "REPLACE_ME",
    "contribution-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "link-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "hunger-games-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "status-channel": ""
  },
  {
    "tag": "de",
    "role": "REPLACE_ME",
    "contribution-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "link-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "hunger-games-channel": "REPLACE_ME",
    "status-channel": ""
  }
]'

# Discord bot - the product
# What can be bought. Leave this unset to use the agreed price list, which is what a fresh
# access.yml is written with: 30 days for 3 EUR, 60 for 5, 90 for 7.
#
# Set it to change the offer without a release. Rules, all checked at startup: at least one
# entry, day counts unique, and more days must cost more. A tier is identified by its day count -
# that is what a purchase button carries - so changing `days` on an existing entry retires that
# tier rather than repricing it.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_TIERS='[
#  {"days": 30, "price-cents": 300},
#  {"days": 60, "price-cents": 500},
#  {"days": 90, "price-cents": 700}
#]'

# The optional surcharge that grants the permanent donor role, in cents. Also the threshold at
# which money left over above an order is read as a donation rather than ignored.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_DONATION_CENTS=500

# Discord bot - timings
# All optional; empty means "use what is in access.yml", which is what these values are.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_REQUEST_TTL_HOURS=24
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_RECENT_PAYMENT_COUNT=50
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_EXPIRY_REMINDER_LEAD_DAYS=3
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLE_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=10

# Leave this empty on a fresh database. The first start stamps its own instant into bot_setting
# and no later start rewrites it. Without a cut-off the first poll books up to 50 historical bunq
# payments - grants, roles, DMs and public thank-yous included.
#
# Set it only to choose a different cut-off deliberately: ISO-8601, UTC, e.g.
# 2026-09-01T00:00:00Z. A value here overrides the stored one without replacing it, so emptying
# this again falls back to the original first-start instant.
#NORDTAL_ACCESS_PAYMENT_WATERMARK=


# Steward - the web interface, the deployer and Caddy
# Everything in this section is written by deploy/nordtal.sh on a first run, including the three
# generated secrets. It is documented here because the file it writes is this file, and because a
# value you have to understand before you can answer it is not a value a script should invent.

# The name the interface answers on, without a scheme and without a trailing slash.
#
# It is chosen once. Caddy gets a certificate for exactly this name; steward-ui hands Discord
# `https://<this>/auth/callback` as its redirect URI and Discord refuses anything else; and WebAuthn
# - which this alpha does not have yet - will bind every registered security key to it, so changing
# it later invalidates all of them.
#
# deploy/nordtal.sh resolves this name and waits until it points at this host, saying what it found,
# rather than carrying on and leaving you with an interface and no certificate.
STEWARD_HOST=steward.dev.nordtal.eu

# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings. A real address that somebody reads - it is the only
# warning you get that the renewal has been failing. Required even without the `steward` profile:
# Caddy guards the game port and reads the same configuration.
STEWARD_ACME_EMAIL=

# Two secrets, and they must not be the same value.
#
#   STEWARD_API_TOKEN       lets steward-ui read containers through steward-worker: state, logs,
#                           image digests, one console line.
#   STEWARD_DEPLOYER_TOKEN  lets steward-ui create containers through steward-deployer.
#
# Those are different privileges, which is why they are two processes and two networks-worth of
# separation; one token for both would make that boundary a comment. Generate each with
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. steward-deployer refuses to start without its one.
STEWARD_API_TOKEN=
STEWARD_DEPLOYER_TOKEN=

# The absolute path of this file on this host.
#
# steward-deployer hands the directory around it to `docker compose --env-file`, because the compose
# file it runs is baked into its own image and has nothing else to interpolate from. It must be
# absolute: a relative path would be resolved against the deployer's project directory, which is
# inside the image.
STEWARD_ENV_FILE=/etc/nordtal/season-2.env

# The directory holding the file above, and the file's own name, separately.
#
# steward-deployer mounts STEWARD_ENV_DIR read-only at /app/env - the directory, not the file. A
# file bind mount follows the inode, not the path: replacing this file the way a rotation does (a
# temp file and a `mv`, not an edit in place) leaves the old, now-deleted inode mounted forever, so
# every container this service recreates afterwards gets values from before the rotation, with
# nothing that fails or looks wrong. A directory bind re-resolves the path on every access instead.
# deploy/nordtal.sh derives both from STEWARD_ENV_FILE and refuses to continue if either has drifted
# from it - neither is ever typed by hand.
STEWARD_ENV_DIR=/etc/nordtal
STEWARD_ENV_FILE_NAME=season-2.env

# The installation directory. Every volume in compose.yml defaults to a
# directory under this one - the four worlds, the four plugins/ folders, the database, the two jar
# caches, every config directory, Caddy's certificates and the backups - so the whole installation
# is one folder that SFTP can reach. deploy/nordtal.sh writes it as the directory it was run in and
# refuses to continue if it ever disagrees with where it is being run from.
#
# It must be absolute, for exactly the reason STEWARD_ENV_FILE above must: steward-deployer runs
# `docker compose --project-directory /app` inside its own image, so a relative bind would resolve
# against a directory that exists only in there.
NORDTAL_DIR=/srv/nordtal

# And the way back, which is per volume and needs none of the lines below to exist. Docker tells a
# bind mount from a named volume by the shape of the string alone: a value with a `/` in it is a
# path and a value without one is a volume name. So setting one of these to `mc-smp` puts that one
# directory back into a Docker volume, and leaving them all unset - which is what a normal
# installation does - puts everything under NORDTAL_DIR.
#
# deploy/dev.env.example sets fourteen of them to volume names, which is the local stack's whole
# difference in storage: a laptop has no use for a world as a directory and `deploy/dev reset`
# throws one away by volume name.
#
#PROXY_DATA=mc-proxy
#LIMBO_DATA=mc-limbo
# The two standbys. Nothing in either is worth keeping: the server directory rebuilds itself on
# first start and steward-worker refills the plugins one after every apply.
#PROXY_STANDBY_DATA=mc-proxy-standby
#LIMBO_STANDBY_DATA=mc-limbo-standby
#PROXY_STANDBY_PLUGINS=mc-proxy-standby-plugins
#LIMBO_STANDBY_PLUGINS=mc-limbo-standby-plugins
#HUNGER_GAMES_DATA=mc-hunger-games
#SMP_DATA=mc-smp
#PROXY_PLUGINS=mc-proxy-plugins
#LIMBO_PLUGINS=mc-limbo-plugins
#HUNGER_GAMES_PLUGINS=mc-hunger-games-plugins
#SMP_PLUGINS=mc-smp-plugins
#POSTGRES_DATA=postgres-data
#STEWARD_BACKUPS=steward-backups
#BOT_CONFIG=bot-config
#BOT_JAR=bot-jar
#STEWARD_WORKER_CONFIG=steward-worker-config
#STEWARD_WORKER_JAR=steward-worker-jar
#STEWARD_UI_CONFIG=steward-ui-config
#BUNQ_CONTEXT=bunq-context
#CADDY_DATA=caddy-data
#CADDY_CONFIG=caddy-config

# The Discord application the interface signs people in with - the bot's application, reused:
# one client id, a second secret, and the scopes are fixed in code
# (`identify`, `guilds.members.read`). That is what lets steward-ui read the roles of whoever is
# signing in with a token of theirs rather than one of the bot's.
#
# The redirect URI to register in the Discord developer portal is exactly:
#     https://${STEWARD_HOST}/auth/callback
#
# Empty means nobody can sign in, and the sign-in page names the missing value instead of failing
# at Discord - so these are not required and the stack comes up without them.
STEWARD_UI_DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=
STEWARD_UI_DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Which guild and which role are not here: the interface reads NORDTAL_ACCESS_GUILD_ID and
# NORDTAL_ACCESS_ROLES_ADMIN, the bot's own values, further up this file. Two processes, two
# volumes, no shared file - but one variable, so they start out agreeing.
#
# Neither is the bot token, for the same reason: compose hands steward-ui the same
# NORDTAL_BOT_TOKEN the bot gets, as NORDTAL_STEWARD_UI_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN. It is read-only and it
# is there for one thing - asking Discord what the guild's roles and channels are called, so the
# configuration editor offers a list to pick from instead of a field to transcribe an
# eighteen-digit id into. Steward never sends a message with it, never changes a role, and never
# puts it in an answer, a log or a browser.
#
# It is a real widening and is worth knowing: whoever reaches that container has the bot. The
# alternative was a second Discord application with its own token - a second thing to rotate and a
# second bot in the member list - for a token that is already on this host, in the service next
# door. Leave NORDTAL_BOT_TOKEN empty and the pickers are text fields again; the ids still work.
